³1ip³265.219.214.4³2ts³21085712391³2version³21³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3*³3text³3<b>Existing Law</b>

Concealed carry is illegal in Ohio until the new bill goes into effect. The relevant section of [http://onlinedocs.andersonpublishing.com/oh/lpExt.dll?f=templates&fn=main-h.htm&cp=PORC Ohio's Revised Code] is [http://onlinedocs.andersonpublishing.com/oh/lpExt.dll?f=FifLink&t=document-frame.htm&l=jump&iid=1913020e.684d5f46.0.0&nid=ceb#JD_292312 Section 2923.12]. To navigate there from the main code page, select "TITLE XXIX CRIMES - PROCEDURE", "CHAPTER 2923 CONSPIRACY, ATTEMPT, AND COMPLICITY; WEAPONS CONTROL; CORRUPT ACTIVITY", "2923.12. Carrying concealed weapons". [http://onlinedocs.andersonpublishing.com/oh/lpExt.dll?f=FifLink&t=document-frame.htm&l=jump&iid=1913020e.684d5f46.0.0&nid=b517#JD_292316 Section 2923.16] covers "Improperly handling firearms in a motor vehicle". "Basic prison terms" are described in [http://onlinedocs.andersonpublishing.com/oh/lpExt.dll?f=FifLink&t=document-frame.htm&l=jump&iid=1913020e.684d5f46.0.0&nid=10fd5#JD_292914 Section 2929.14]. "Guidance by degree of felony", [http://onlinedocs.andersonpublishing.com/oh/lpExt.dll?f=FifLink&t=document-frame.htm&l=jump&iid=1913020e.684d5f46.0.0&nid=10fed#JD_292913 2929.13], is a very confusing description of additional fines and penalties.

Summary: Carrying a concealed, loaded firearm is a felony in the fourth degree. This has a prison term of 6 to 18 months. The newspapers are reporting that Hunter's "offense" could get 3 years and $10,000, so I assume they're thinking that he'll get two concealed weapons charges, one for each handgun. There is an affirmative defense that "The weapon was carried or kept ready at hand by the actor for defensive purposes, while the actor was engaged in a lawful activity and had reasonable cause to fear a criminal attack upon the actor or a member of the actor's family, or upon the actor's home, such as would justify a prudent person in going armed", but we'd need to know case history to determine when this defense is effective, i.e. what "reasonable cause to fear a criminal attack" and "justify a prudent person" exactly mean.

Firearms carried in a vehicle must be unloaded and in one of the following places: "(1) In a closed package, box, or case; (2) In a compartment that can be reached only by leaving the vehicle; (3) In plain sight and secured in a rack or holder made for the purpose; (4) In plain sight with the action open or the weapon stripped, or, if the firearm is of a type on which the action will not stay open or which cannot easily be stripped, in plain sight."

<b>New CCW Law</b>

On January 8, Governor Taft signed into law [http://www.legislature.state.oh.us/bills.cfm?ID=125_HB_0012%22%3EHB%200012 HB 0012], which provides shall-issue permits for concealed carry of handguns. This law goes into affect on April 2, Hunter's birthday, but most commentators I've read think it will be July before any permits are delivered.

A permit holder may carry a loaded handgun in a motor vehicle (search for "holster" in the longwinded text of the bill) if it is in a holster in plain sight and if the permit holder does not intentionally touch it unless directed to do so by a cop. If stopped by a cop, the permit holder must tell him about the permit and the loaded handgun, keep his hands in plain view, stay in the vehicle, and follow all lawful orders. Carrying a loaded gun in a motor vehicle without a permit remains a felony in the fourth degree, but the affirmative defenses that used to be included are no more.

The new law directs the attorney general to enter into reciprocity agreements with other states having "substantially comparable" eligibility requirements and that recognize Ohio permits. No telling how long this will take or which states will be determined to have "substantially comparable" eligibility requirements.

